1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,157
It's a Saturday in December,

2
00:00:02,157 --> 00:00:03,622
nineteen sixty-two.

3
00:00:03,802 --> 00:00:05,485
Downtown is packed.

4
00:00:05,665 --> 00:00:08,807
Shoppers in wool coats and hats crowd

5
00:00:08,807 --> 00:00:09,996
the sidewalks.

6
00:00:10,176 --> 00:00:12,817
A Salvation Army bell is ringing on

7
00:00:12,817 --> 00:00:13,647
the corner.

8
00:00:13,827 --> 00:00:15,754
And in the middle of the main

9
00:00:15,754 --> 00:00:18,612
street stands the biggest building in town,

10
00:00:18,612 --> 00:00:21,004
with tall windows full of lights and

11
00:00:21,004 --> 00:00:22,001
moving figures.

12
00:00:22,181 --> 00:00:23,504
The department store.

13
00:00:23,855 --> 00:00:25,669
In nineteen sixty-two,

14
00:00:25,669 --> 00:00:29,051
almost every American city and many small

15
00:00:29,051 --> 00:00:32,103
towns had at least one big department

16
00:00:32,103 --> 00:00:33,340
store downtown.

17
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:34,513
In New York,

18
00:00:34,513 --> 00:00:36,086
Macy's and Gimbels.

19
00:00:36,266 --> 00:00:38,681
In Chicago, Marshall Field's.

20
00:00:38,861 --> 00:00:41,450
In Philadelphia, Wanamaker's.

21
00:00:41,630 --> 00:00:43,685
In Detroit, Hudson's.

22
00:00:43,865 --> 00:00:45,982
And in hundreds of smaller cities,

23
00:00:45,982 --> 00:00:48,722
a family-owned store with the founder's name

24
00:00:48,722 --> 00:00:50,029
carved over the door.

25
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:52,214
So let's go shopping.

26
00:00:52,394 --> 00:00:54,556
Let's walk through the doors of a

27
00:00:54,556 --> 00:00:57,701
downtown department store in nineteen sixty-two,

28
00:00:57,701 --> 00:01:00,191
and ride the elevator from the bargain

29
00:01:00,191 --> 00:01:02,157
basement all the way up to the

30
00:01:02,157 --> 00:01:03,795
tearoom on the top floor.

31
00:01:03,975 --> 00:01:06,040
If you remember those stores,

32
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:08,389
I think you'll feel that warm air

33
00:01:08,389 --> 00:01:10,952
hit you as the revolving door spins.

34
00:01:11,302 --> 00:01:13,221
Before you even went inside,

35
00:01:13,221 --> 00:01:14,797
there were the windows.

36
00:01:14,977 --> 00:01:18,285
In December, the big department stores competed

37
00:01:18,285 --> 00:01:21,241
to have the most magical Christmas windows

38
00:01:21,241 --> 00:01:21,804
in town.

39
00:01:21,984 --> 00:01:24,608
Mechanical elves hammered toys.

40
00:01:24,788 --> 00:01:27,632
Animated bears skated on mirror ponds.

41
00:01:27,812 --> 00:01:30,227
Snow fell on tiny villages.

42
00:01:30,407 --> 00:01:33,274
Families came downtown just to walk past

43
00:01:33,274 --> 00:01:36,500
the windows, and children pressed their noses

44
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:37,791
against the glass.

45
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:40,184
Window dressers were artists.

46
00:01:40,364 --> 00:01:42,447
They worked for months on the holiday

47
00:01:42,447 --> 00:01:44,869
displays, and changed the windows every few

48
00:01:44,869 --> 00:01:46,389
weeks the rest of the year.

49
00:01:46,569 --> 00:01:49,690
Spring fashions, back-to-school clothes,

50
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:51,016
summer beachwear.

51
00:01:51,196 --> 00:01:52,372
For many shoppers,

52
00:01:52,372 --> 00:01:54,659
the window was the first place they

53
00:01:54,659 --> 00:01:56,815
saw what was new and fashionable.

54
00:01:57,165 --> 00:01:59,881
Some stores also put on a Thanksgiving

55
00:01:59,881 --> 00:02:00,381
parade.

56
00:02:00,561 --> 00:02:02,940
Macy's in New York had been holding

57
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:05,522
its parade since nineteen twenty-four,

58
00:02:05,522 --> 00:02:08,445
with giant balloons floating down Broadway.

59
00:02:08,625 --> 00:02:10,051
Hudson's in Detroit,

60
00:02:10,051 --> 00:02:13,262
Gimbels in Philadelphia and many other stores

61
00:02:13,262 --> 00:02:15,045
had parades of their own.

62
00:02:15,225 --> 00:02:17,608
At the very end came Santa Claus,

63
00:02:17,608 --> 00:02:20,859
which meant the Christmas shopping season had

64
00:02:20,859 --> 00:02:22,086
officially begun.

65
00:02:22,437 --> 00:02:24,192
Through the revolving doors,

66
00:02:24,192 --> 00:02:26,198
you stepped onto the main floor.

67
00:02:26,378 --> 00:02:27,320
It was grand,

68
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:28,697
with high ceilings,

69
00:02:28,697 --> 00:02:32,393
marble columns, chandeliers and glass counters that

70
00:02:32,393 --> 00:02:32,973
gleamed.

71
00:02:33,153 --> 00:02:34,722
It smelled of perfume,

72
00:02:34,722 --> 00:02:36,578
leather and fresh popcorn.

73
00:02:36,758 --> 00:02:38,806
The main floor was where the store

74
00:02:38,806 --> 00:02:41,216
put the things people bought on impulse.

75
00:02:41,396 --> 00:02:44,333
Cosmetics, jewelry, gloves,

76
00:02:44,333 --> 00:02:47,270
hats, scarves and handbags.

77
00:02:47,620 --> 00:02:50,256
Behind every counter stood a saleswoman in

78
00:02:50,256 --> 00:02:51,072
a neat dress,

79
00:02:51,072 --> 00:02:53,206
often a woman who had worked there

80
00:02:53,206 --> 00:02:54,273
for twenty years.

81
00:02:54,453 --> 00:02:57,251
She knew her merchandise and her customers.

82
00:02:57,431 --> 00:02:59,807
She'd help you find the right shade

83
00:02:59,807 --> 00:03:02,115
of lipstick, the right size glove,

84
00:03:02,115 --> 00:03:04,560
or the perfect gift for your mother.

85
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:06,690
She wrote up the sale by hand,

86
00:03:06,690 --> 00:03:08,966
in a little book with carbon paper.

87
00:03:09,316 --> 00:03:10,205
When you paid,

88
00:03:10,205 --> 00:03:12,364
the clerk might put your money and

89
00:03:12,364 --> 00:03:14,715
the sales slip into a little cylinder

90
00:03:14,715 --> 00:03:17,319
and send it whooshing through a pneumatic

91
00:03:17,319 --> 00:03:19,161
tube to the cashier's office.

92
00:03:19,341 --> 00:03:20,333
A minute later,

93
00:03:20,333 --> 00:03:22,912
it came whooshing back with your change

94
00:03:22,912 --> 00:03:23,706
and receipt.

95
00:03:23,886 --> 00:03:25,918
Children were fascinated by it.

96
00:03:26,098 --> 00:03:28,844
Some stores used those tube systems well

97
00:03:28,844 --> 00:03:30,010
into the sixties.

98
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:33,060
Many stores also had a beautiful clock,

99
00:03:33,060 --> 00:03:35,552
often hanging over the main floor or

100
00:03:35,552 --> 00:03:37,628
at the corner of the building.

101
00:03:37,808 --> 00:03:39,618
Meet me under the clock was a

102
00:03:39,618 --> 00:03:41,802
phrase everyone in town understood.

103
00:03:41,982 --> 00:03:45,114
Friends, sweethearts and families used it as

104
00:03:45,114 --> 00:03:47,392
a meeting place for generations.

105
00:03:47,572 --> 00:03:50,124
Marshall Field's clock in Chicago was so

106
00:03:50,124 --> 00:03:52,421
famous that it appeared on the cover

107
00:03:52,421 --> 00:03:54,271
of the Saturday Evening Post.

108
00:03:54,621 --> 00:03:55,902
To go upstairs,

109
00:03:55,902 --> 00:03:57,779
you took the elevator.

110
00:03:57,959 --> 00:03:59,916
And in nineteen sixty-two,

111
00:03:59,916 --> 00:04:03,227
many department store elevators still had an

112
00:04:03,227 --> 00:04:03,904
operator.

113
00:04:04,084 --> 00:04:06,256
A young woman or an older man

114
00:04:06,256 --> 00:04:08,578
in a uniform with white gloves,

115
00:04:08,578 --> 00:04:10,525
who sat on a little stool,

116
00:04:10,525 --> 00:04:12,098
worked a brass lever,

117
00:04:12,098 --> 00:04:14,045
and called out each floor.

118
00:04:14,225 --> 00:04:16,659
Third floor, ladies' dresses,

119
00:04:16,659 --> 00:04:18,254
coats and lingerie.

120
00:04:18,434 --> 00:04:19,037
Going up.

121
00:04:19,387 --> 00:04:21,626
Some stores had escalators too,

122
00:04:21,626 --> 00:04:24,515
with wooden steps that clattered as they

123
00:04:24,515 --> 00:04:24,949
moved.

124
00:04:25,129 --> 00:04:27,578
Children loved riding them up and down.

125
00:04:27,758 --> 00:04:30,442
Mothers held small hands tightly at the

126
00:04:30,442 --> 00:04:31,474
top and bottom.

127
00:04:31,654 --> 00:04:34,166
And the escalator always seemed to deliver

128
00:04:34,166 --> 00:04:36,439
you right into the middle of something

129
00:04:36,439 --> 00:04:37,935
you didn't intend to buy.

130
00:04:38,284 --> 00:04:40,571
The second and third floors were the

131
00:04:40,571 --> 00:04:41,524
fashion floors.

132
00:04:41,704 --> 00:04:44,044
Women's dresses, suits,

133
00:04:44,044 --> 00:04:45,570
coats and furs.

134
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:47,449
In nineteen sixty-two,

135
00:04:47,449 --> 00:04:49,070
the look was elegant.

136
00:04:49,250 --> 00:04:52,382
Jackie Kennedy was the most admired woman

137
00:04:52,382 --> 00:04:55,667
in America, and women everywhere wanted her

138
00:04:55,667 --> 00:04:59,258
pillbox hats, simple sheath dresses and pearls.

139
00:04:59,438 --> 00:05:02,381
Stores sold copies of her outfits at

140
00:05:02,381 --> 00:05:03,362
every price.

141
00:05:03,712 --> 00:05:06,603
Shopping for clothes was a serious event.

142
00:05:06,783 --> 00:05:09,415
Women dressed up to go downtown,

143
00:05:09,415 --> 00:05:11,718
with hats, gloves and heels.

144
00:05:11,898 --> 00:05:14,871
Fitting rooms had attendants who brought you

145
00:05:14,871 --> 00:05:15,276
sizes.

146
00:05:15,456 --> 00:05:18,024
Alterations were done in the store,

147
00:05:18,024 --> 00:05:19,125
often for free.

148
00:05:19,305 --> 00:05:21,988
And many stores had a personal shopper,

149
00:05:21,988 --> 00:05:24,396
who could pick out a whole wardrobe

150
00:05:24,396 --> 00:05:25,772
for a busy customer.

151
00:05:26,121 --> 00:05:28,785
The men's department was often on the

152
00:05:28,785 --> 00:05:31,160
main floor or in a separate shop.

153
00:05:31,340 --> 00:05:33,274
Suits, sport coats,

154
00:05:33,274 --> 00:05:36,123
white shirts, ties and hats.

155
00:05:36,303 --> 00:05:38,302
A tailor measured a man for his

156
00:05:38,302 --> 00:05:40,236
suit with a tape measure and a

157
00:05:40,236 --> 00:05:41,203
piece of chalk.

158
00:05:41,383 --> 00:05:43,079
In nineteen sixty-two,

159
00:05:43,079 --> 00:05:45,469
a good suit might cost fifty to

160
00:05:45,469 --> 00:05:48,862
seventy-five dollars, and a man was expected

161
00:05:48,862 --> 00:05:50,404
to own at least two.

162
00:05:50,753 --> 00:05:52,890
Hats were a big business.

163
00:05:53,070 --> 00:05:54,704
In nineteen sixty-two,

164
00:05:54,704 --> 00:05:57,155
many women still bought a new hat

165
00:05:57,155 --> 00:06:00,571
every season, and the millinery department was

166
00:06:00,571 --> 00:06:01,834
full of feathers,

167
00:06:01,834 --> 00:06:03,170
veils and flowers.

168
00:06:03,350 --> 00:06:05,053
Men still wore fedoras,

169
00:06:05,053 --> 00:06:07,275
though that was changing fast.

170
00:06:07,455 --> 00:06:09,791
People said President Kennedy,

171
00:06:09,791 --> 00:06:12,050
who often went without a hat,

172
00:06:12,050 --> 00:06:14,698
helped bring the era of men's hats

173
00:06:14,698 --> 00:06:15,477
to an end.

174
00:06:15,826 --> 00:06:19,077
Children's clothing was usually on its own

175
00:06:19,077 --> 00:06:20,935
floor, along with shoes.

176
00:06:21,115 --> 00:06:24,151
Mothers came in late August for back-to-school

177
00:06:24,151 --> 00:06:24,679
clothes.

178
00:06:24,859 --> 00:06:27,738
Plaid dresses and saddle shoes for girls.

179
00:06:27,918 --> 00:06:31,158
Corduroy pants and button-down shirts for boys.

180
00:06:31,338 --> 00:06:33,797
And the shoe salesman measured your foot

181
00:06:33,797 --> 00:06:35,580
with a metal Brannock device,

182
00:06:35,580 --> 00:06:37,363
and maybe gave you a balloon.

183
00:06:37,713 --> 00:06:39,919
And then there was the toy department.

184
00:06:40,099 --> 00:06:42,398
In December, it took over a whole

185
00:06:42,398 --> 00:06:42,816
floor.

186
00:06:42,996 --> 00:06:45,912
Electric trains running on tracks around a

187
00:06:45,912 --> 00:06:46,537
mountain.

188
00:06:46,717 --> 00:06:48,877
Dolls in every size.

189
00:06:49,057 --> 00:06:53,213
Bicycles, sleds, games and chemistry sets.

190
00:06:53,393 --> 00:06:55,616
For a child in nineteen sixty-two,

191
00:06:55,616 --> 00:06:58,362
the toy department at Christmas might have

192
00:06:58,362 --> 00:07:00,847
been the most exciting place on Earth.

193
00:07:01,196 --> 00:07:03,020
And in the middle of it all

194
00:07:03,020 --> 00:07:04,101
sat Santa Claus,

195
00:07:04,101 --> 00:07:05,925
on a big throne in a little

196
00:07:05,925 --> 00:07:07,141
winter wonderland.

197
00:07:07,321 --> 00:07:09,399
The line to see him wound through

198
00:07:09,399 --> 00:07:10,595
the toy department.

199
00:07:10,775 --> 00:07:12,816
Children whispered their wishes,

200
00:07:12,816 --> 00:07:13,900
got a candy cane,

201
00:07:13,900 --> 00:07:15,686
and had their picture taken.

202
00:07:15,866 --> 00:07:18,649
Many families still have those photos,

203
00:07:18,649 --> 00:07:21,286
with a nervous child on Santa's lap,

204
00:07:21,286 --> 00:07:23,923
from the same store year after year.

205
00:07:24,273 --> 00:07:26,514
Higher up were the home floors.

206
00:07:26,694 --> 00:07:30,224
Housewares, china, silver and linens.

207
00:07:30,404 --> 00:07:33,233
Brides registered for their wedding gifts here,

208
00:07:33,233 --> 00:07:35,641
choosing a china pattern and silver they

209
00:07:35,641 --> 00:07:37,508
would use for the rest of their

210
00:07:37,508 --> 00:07:37,869
lives.

211
00:07:38,049 --> 00:07:40,647
Furniture filled whole showrooms,

212
00:07:40,647 --> 00:07:43,796
arranged like living rooms and bedrooms.

213
00:07:43,976 --> 00:07:47,873
Appliances, televisions and stereos had their own

214
00:07:47,873 --> 00:07:48,747
department.

215
00:07:49,097 --> 00:07:51,957
Many families bought their first good furniture

216
00:07:51,957 --> 00:07:53,296
at the downtown store,

217
00:07:53,296 --> 00:07:53,904
on credit.

218
00:07:54,084 --> 00:07:56,186
And that credit came with a charge

219
00:07:56,186 --> 00:07:56,557
plate.

220
00:07:56,737 --> 00:07:58,148
A little metal card,

221
00:07:58,148 --> 00:07:59,206
like a dog tag,

222
00:07:59,206 --> 00:08:02,240
embossed with your name and account number.

223
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:04,700
The clerk pressed it into a machine

224
00:08:04,700 --> 00:08:06,263
to stamp the sales slip.

225
00:08:06,443 --> 00:08:08,663
Having a charge account at the big

226
00:08:08,663 --> 00:08:10,884
store downtown was a sign that you

227
00:08:10,884 --> 00:08:11,667
had arrived.

228
00:08:12,017 --> 00:08:14,138
And for families who couldn't pay all

229
00:08:14,138 --> 00:08:15,686
at once, there was layaway.

230
00:08:15,866 --> 00:08:17,636
You picked out a coat or a

231
00:08:17,636 --> 00:08:19,541
toy, put down a few dollars,

232
00:08:19,541 --> 00:08:21,447
and the store held it in the

233
00:08:21,447 --> 00:08:21,787
back.

234
00:08:21,967 --> 00:08:23,330
Every week or two,

235
00:08:23,330 --> 00:08:25,525
you came in and paid a little

236
00:08:25,525 --> 00:08:25,903
more.

237
00:08:26,083 --> 00:08:27,582
When it was paid off,

238
00:08:27,582 --> 00:08:28,510
it was yours.

239
00:08:28,690 --> 00:08:31,534
Many Christmas presents came home that way.

240
00:08:31,883 --> 00:08:34,913
Many stores also offered services that sound

241
00:08:34,913 --> 00:08:35,877
amazing today.

242
00:08:36,057 --> 00:08:39,024
Free delivery, often the same day or

243
00:08:39,024 --> 00:08:41,990
the next, by the store's own trucks.

244
00:08:42,170 --> 00:08:44,668
Gift wrapping at a special counter,

245
00:08:44,668 --> 00:08:47,023
with the store's name on the box.

246
00:08:47,203 --> 00:08:48,540
A beauty salon,

247
00:08:48,540 --> 00:08:49,788
a barber shop,

248
00:08:49,788 --> 00:08:51,035
a post office,

249
00:08:51,035 --> 00:08:53,709
a travel bureau and a place to

250
00:08:53,709 --> 00:08:56,294
check your coat and packages.

251
00:08:56,474 --> 00:08:58,215
You could spend the whole day there.

252
00:08:58,565 --> 00:09:00,979
At lunchtime, shoppers rode up to the

253
00:09:00,979 --> 00:09:02,675
top floor, to the tearoom.

254
00:09:02,855 --> 00:09:04,001
It was elegant,

255
00:09:04,001 --> 00:09:05,758
with white tablecloths,

256
00:09:05,758 --> 00:09:07,515
waitresses in uniforms,

257
00:09:07,515 --> 00:09:10,418
and sometimes a fashion show of models

258
00:09:10,418 --> 00:09:12,480
walking between the tables.

259
00:09:12,660 --> 00:09:16,160
Women met their friends for chicken salad,

260
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:19,244
tomato aspic, popovers and a slice of

261
00:09:19,244 --> 00:09:19,661
cake.

262
00:09:19,841 --> 00:09:22,529
Some stores had a children's menu with

263
00:09:22,529 --> 00:09:24,438
sandwiches cut into shapes.

264
00:09:24,788 --> 00:09:27,587
Many stores were famous for one special

265
00:09:27,587 --> 00:09:27,946
dish.

266
00:09:28,126 --> 00:09:31,540
Marshall Field's in Chicago served chicken pot

267
00:09:31,540 --> 00:09:33,247
pie in its Walnut Room,

268
00:09:33,247 --> 00:09:35,325
under a huge Christmas tree.

269
00:09:35,505 --> 00:09:39,046
Neiman Marcus served popovers with strawberry butter.

270
00:09:39,226 --> 00:09:41,838
Hudson's in Detroit had its Maurice salad.

271
00:09:42,018 --> 00:09:45,126
People still try to recreate those recipes

272
00:09:45,126 --> 00:09:46,163
at home today.

273
00:09:46,512 --> 00:09:48,742
And then there was the bargain basement.

274
00:09:48,922 --> 00:09:50,531
Down below the main floor,

275
00:09:50,531 --> 00:09:51,894
things were different.

276
00:09:52,074 --> 00:09:54,446
Lower prices, crowded tables,

277
00:09:54,446 --> 00:09:57,472
and piles of marked-down merchandise.

278
00:09:57,652 --> 00:10:00,715
Filene's Basement in Boston was the most

279
00:10:00,715 --> 00:10:01,251
famous.

280
00:10:01,431 --> 00:10:04,965
There, prices dropped automatically every week an

281
00:10:04,965 --> 00:10:06,191
item went unsold,

282
00:10:06,191 --> 00:10:09,292
and shoppers fought over wedding dresses on

283
00:10:09,292 --> 00:10:09,942
sale day.

284
00:10:10,291 --> 00:10:11,836
For many working families,

285
00:10:11,836 --> 00:10:14,390
the basement was where they really shopped.

286
00:10:14,570 --> 00:10:16,434
Clothes for the children,

287
00:10:16,434 --> 00:10:18,970
towels, socks and everyday things.

288
00:10:19,150 --> 00:10:21,854
And everyone loved the thrill of finding

289
00:10:21,854 --> 00:10:23,747
a real treasure in the bins.

290
00:10:23,927 --> 00:10:26,480
A designer coat for a fraction of

291
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:29,187
the price, or the perfect shoes for

292
00:10:29,187 --> 00:10:29,883
half off.

293
00:10:30,233 --> 00:10:32,312
And the store's own box mattered.

294
00:10:32,492 --> 00:10:34,993
A gift in a Marshall Field's green

295
00:10:34,993 --> 00:10:37,126
box, a Neiman Marcus box or a

296
00:10:37,126 --> 00:10:40,068
Tiffany blue box told the person opening

297
00:10:40,068 --> 00:10:42,054
it that you had gone to the

298
00:10:42,054 --> 00:10:43,451
best store in town.

299
00:10:43,631 --> 00:10:46,391
Many people kept those boxes for years

300
00:10:46,391 --> 00:10:47,553
and reused them,

301
00:10:47,553 --> 00:10:50,094
so that even a homemade gift seemed

302
00:10:50,094 --> 00:10:51,329
a little fancier.

303
00:10:51,678 --> 00:10:54,160
The department store was the heart of

304
00:10:54,160 --> 00:10:56,775
downtown, and downtown was the heart of

305
00:10:56,775 --> 00:10:57,379
the city.

306
00:10:57,559 --> 00:10:58,654
On a Saturday,

307
00:10:58,654 --> 00:11:01,626
families took the bus or the streetcar

308
00:11:01,626 --> 00:11:04,051
into town, or parked in the new

309
00:11:04,051 --> 00:11:05,303
parking garages.

310
00:11:05,483 --> 00:11:07,256
They shopped, ate lunch,

311
00:11:07,256 --> 00:11:09,546
saw a movie at the big theater,

312
00:11:09,546 --> 00:11:12,057
and maybe stopped at the bakery on

313
00:11:12,057 --> 00:11:13,018
the way home.

314
00:11:13,367 --> 00:11:16,200
Every shopping trip was a social event.

315
00:11:16,380 --> 00:11:17,728
You ran into neighbors,

316
00:11:17,728 --> 00:11:20,130
friends from church and people from work.

317
00:11:20,310 --> 00:11:22,193
The doorman at the store knew your

318
00:11:22,193 --> 00:11:22,470
name.

319
00:11:22,650 --> 00:11:24,834
The streets were full of life,

320
00:11:24,834 --> 00:11:25,854
day and night.

321
00:11:26,034 --> 00:11:27,664
In nineteen sixty-two,

322
00:11:27,664 --> 00:11:30,701
going downtown felt like going somewhere.

323
00:11:31,051 --> 00:11:32,119
So what happened?

324
00:11:32,299 --> 00:11:34,486
The same thing that happened to Main

325
00:11:34,486 --> 00:11:34,912
Street.

326
00:11:35,092 --> 00:11:37,391
Families moved to the suburbs.

327
00:11:37,571 --> 00:11:40,243
Shopping malls opened on the edge of

328
00:11:40,243 --> 00:11:43,584
town, with free parking and air conditioning.

329
00:11:43,764 --> 00:11:46,858
Many department stores opened branches in the

330
00:11:46,858 --> 00:11:47,271
malls.

331
00:11:47,451 --> 00:11:51,155
And slowly, the downtown flagship lost its

332
00:11:51,155 --> 00:11:52,037
customers.

333
00:11:52,386 --> 00:11:55,000
Through the seventies and eighties,

334
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,390
many big downtown stores closed.

335
00:11:57,570 --> 00:12:00,635
Gimbels closed in nineteen eighty-six.

336
00:12:00,815 --> 00:12:04,357
Hudson's downtown Detroit store closed in nineteen

337
00:12:04,357 --> 00:12:07,616
eighty-three, and its building was imploded in

338
00:12:07,616 --> 00:12:09,175
nineteen ninety-eight.

339
00:12:09,355 --> 00:12:10,690
Then came mergers.

340
00:12:10,870 --> 00:12:12,851
Many beloved local names,

341
00:12:12,851 --> 00:12:14,595
like Marshall Field's,

342
00:12:14,595 --> 00:12:17,210
Filene's, Rich's and Wanamaker's,

343
00:12:17,210 --> 00:12:19,113
were turned into Macy's.

344
00:12:19,462 --> 00:12:22,713
Today, many of those grand old buildings

345
00:12:22,713 --> 00:12:25,720
stand empty, or have been turned into

346
00:12:25,720 --> 00:12:28,158
apartments, offices or hotels.

347
00:12:28,338 --> 00:12:30,941
A few, like Macy's in Herald Square

348
00:12:30,941 --> 00:12:31,833
in New York,

349
00:12:31,833 --> 00:12:34,584
still open their doors every morning.

350
00:12:34,764 --> 00:12:35,872
And at Christmas,

351
00:12:35,872 --> 00:12:38,086
some still decorate their windows,

352
00:12:38,086 --> 00:12:40,627
and families still come downtown to see

353
00:12:40,627 --> 00:12:40,953
them.

354
00:12:41,302 --> 00:12:42,986
Why do we miss them so much?

355
00:12:43,166 --> 00:12:46,385
Because the downtown department store was more

356
00:12:46,385 --> 00:12:48,274
than a place to buy things.

357
00:12:48,454 --> 00:12:50,323
It was a place to dress up,

358
00:12:50,323 --> 00:12:51,638
to see and be seen,

359
00:12:51,638 --> 00:12:53,991
to take the children to see Santa,

360
00:12:53,991 --> 00:12:56,344
and to have lunch with your mother

361
00:12:56,344 --> 00:12:57,382
in the tearoom.

362
00:12:57,562 --> 00:13:00,523
It made an ordinary Saturday feel special.

363
00:13:00,872 --> 00:13:03,778
If you remember the downtown department store,

364
00:13:03,778 --> 00:13:05,989
you remember the elevator operator,

365
00:13:05,989 --> 00:13:07,379
the Christmas windows,

366
00:13:07,379 --> 00:13:09,464
the charge plate and the tearoom.

367
00:13:09,644 --> 00:13:12,047
You remember holding your mother's hand on

368
00:13:12,047 --> 00:13:12,848
the escalator.

369
00:13:13,028 --> 00:13:14,839
Those stores are mostly gone.

370
00:13:15,019 --> 00:13:17,016
But for a generation of Americans,

371
00:13:17,016 --> 00:13:19,071
they were where the magic happened.

372
00:13:19,421 --> 00:13:21,186
So tell us in the comments.

373
00:13:21,366 --> 00:13:23,409
What was the name of your downtown

374
00:13:23,409 --> 00:13:24,431
department store?

375
00:13:24,611 --> 00:13:26,297
Did you visit Santa there,

376
00:13:26,297 --> 00:13:27,723
or eat in the tearoom?

377
00:13:27,903 --> 00:13:29,656
What did your family buy there?

378
00:13:29,836 --> 00:13:31,368
We read every comment.

379
00:13:31,548 --> 00:13:34,185
Next time, we're looking at what being

380
00:13:34,185 --> 00:13:37,099
a grandparent in nineteen sixty was really

381
00:13:37,099 --> 00:13:37,446
like.

382
00:13:37,626 --> 00:13:39,398
Thank you for spending this time with

383
00:13:39,398 --> 00:13:39,542
me.
